# Questions beginners often ask.
FAQ = {
 1: [("Is machine learning the same as AI?", "No. Artificial intelligence is the broad goal of making computers do intelligent tasks. Machine learning is one approach to it — learning from data — and today it's the most successful one. Deep learning (Lesson 19) is a part of machine learning."),
     ("How much data do I need?", "It depends on how complex the pattern is. A straight line can be fitted from a handful of points; recognising objects in photos needs thousands to millions of images. Lesson 5 shows how to check whether you have enough: if the model overfits, more data usually helps."),
     ("Do I need to be good at maths?", "You need to be comfortable with basic algebra. Lessons 2–4 teach the rest from scratch, and every formula is explained with a worked example.")],
 2: [("Why use matrices instead of loops?", "They describe operations on all examples at once in one short expression, and libraries like NumPy run them in fast compiled code — often hundreds of times faster than a Python loop."),
     ("What's the difference between a vector and a list?", "In code they look alike. Mathematically, a vector supports operations like addition, scaling and dot products, and has a length and a direction."),
     ("Do I have to multiply matrices by hand?", "No — NumPy's <code>@</code> does it. Doing a few small ones by hand builds the intuition you need to read formulas and fix shape errors.")],
 3: [("Why do we need derivatives at all?", "To know which direction to move each parameter to reduce the loss. Without them you'd have to guess, which becomes hopeless with thousands of parameters."),
     ("Will I have to differentiate everything by hand?", "No. Libraries compute gradients automatically. Understanding what a gradient means is what lets you choose learning rates and diagnose training problems."),
     ("Why does gradient descent subtract the gradient?", "The gradient points uphill (towards higher loss), so stepping in the opposite direction goes downhill.")],
 4: [("Why does ML care about probability?", "Because data is noisy and predictions are uncertain. Outputs like \"92% likely spam\" are more useful than a bare yes/no, and many models are built on probability rules."),
     ("Mean or median?", "Use the median when data is skewed or has outliers (incomes, house prices). Use the mean when data is roughly symmetric."),
     ("Is the normal distribution always the right assumption?", "No. It fits many measurements, but counts, waiting times, incomes and yes/no outcomes follow other distributions. Always look at your data.")],
 5: [("How big should the test set be?", "Big enough that its score is stable — often 10–30% of the data. With millions of examples, 1% may be plenty."),
     ("Can I ever look at the test set?", "Only at the very end, once. If you change the model after seeing the test score, that score is no longer an honest estimate."),
     ("What k should I use for cross-validation?", "5 or 10 are common defaults. Larger k uses more data for training each time but costs more runs.")],
 6: [("Why square the errors instead of using absolute values?", "Squared error is smooth, gives a closed-form solution and punishes large errors more. Absolute error (the basis of MAE) is more robust to outliers but harder to optimise; both are valid choices."),
     ("Can linear regression fit curves?", "Yes, by adding features such as $x^2$ or $x^3$. The model stays linear in its weights, so the same maths works."),
     ("What if two features are almost the same?", "Their weights become unstable and hard to interpret. Remove one, combine them, or use regularization (Lesson 10).")],
 7: [("How do I choose the learning rate?", "Try values spaced by factors of 10 (0.001, 0.01, 0.1…) and plot the loss curve. Pick the largest one that decreases smoothly."),
     ("How do I know when to stop?", "When the loss stops improving, or — better — when the validation loss starts rising (early stopping)."),
     ("Can gradient descent get stuck?", "For convex losses like linear and logistic regression, no. For neural networks it can reach local minima or flat regions; in practice the results are usually still good.")],
 8: [("Why is it called regression if it classifies?", "Historical naming: it regresses (fits) the log-odds of the class with a linear function. It's used as a classifier."),
     ("Are its probabilities trustworthy?", "Often reasonably well calibrated, especially compared with many other models — but always check on held-out data."),
     ("What if classes aren't linearly separable?", "Add features (squares, products) or use a non-linear model: trees, kNN, SVM kernels or neural networks.")],
 9: [("Which single metric should I report?", "The one that matches the real cost of mistakes. If unsure, report several: precision, recall, F1 and ROC-AUC — plus a baseline."),
     ("Why is my accuracy high but the model useless?", "Probably class imbalance: predicting the majority class already gives high accuracy. Look at recall and precision for the minority class."),
     ("ROC-AUC or precision–recall?", "ROC-AUC for balanced classes; precision–recall (average precision) when positives are rare.")],
 10: [("How do I know if I have high bias or high variance?", "Compare training and validation error. Both high and close → bias. Training much lower than validation → variance."),
      ("Does regularization always help?", "No. If the model underfits, regularization makes it worse. That's why its strength is tuned with cross-validation."),
      ("Ridge or lasso?", "Ridge when most features matter a bit; lasso when you expect only a few to matter or want automatic feature selection. ElasticNet mixes both.")],
 11: [("How do I choose k?", "Try a range (e.g. 1–50) with cross-validation and pick the best. Odd numbers avoid ties for two classes."),
      ("Why is kNN slow to predict?", "It compares each new point with every training example. Tree indexes (KD-tree, ball tree) speed this up in low dimensions."),
      ("Does kNN learn anything?", "Not in the usual sense — it stores the data. All the work happens at prediction time, which is why it's called a lazy learner.")],
 12: [("If the independence assumption is wrong, why does it work?", "Because classification only needs the correct class to score highest. Errors in the exact probabilities often don't change which class wins."),
      ("Which variant should I use?", "Multinomial for word counts, Bernoulli for present/absent features, Gaussian for continuous measurements."),
      ("Is Naive Bayes still used today?", "Yes — as a fast baseline, for text classification with little data, and wherever training must be nearly instant.")],
 13: [("Gini or entropy?", "They usually give very similar trees. Gini is scikit-learn's default and slightly faster to compute."),
      ("Why don't trees need scaling?", "Each split compares one feature with a threshold, so changing a feature's units just changes the threshold."),
      ("How deep should a tree be?", "Choose max_depth (or min_samples_leaf) by cross-validation; shallow trees for explanation, deeper ones inside ensembles.")],
 14: [("Random forest or gradient boosting?", "Random forests work well with almost no tuning. Gradient boosting is often slightly more accurate but needs its learning rate and number of trees tuned."),
      ("How many trees?", "For random forests, hundreds is typical — more never overfits, it only costs time. For boosting, choose with early stopping or cross-validation."),
      ("Can I still interpret an ensemble?", "Less directly than one tree, but feature importances and tools such as permutation importance and partial dependence plots help.")],
 15: [("What does C actually control?", "The trade-off between a wide margin and classifying every training point correctly. Small C = wider margin, more tolerance of errors (more regularization)."),
      ("Which kernel should I start with?", "RBF is the usual default. Try linear first if you have very many features, such as text."),
      ("Why are SVMs less popular than they were?", "Gradient boosting tends to win on tabular data and deep learning on images and text; kernel SVMs also scale poorly to very large datasets. They remain a solid choice for medium-sized problems.")],
 16: [("How do I know the clusters are meaningful?", "Check silhouette scores, look at examples from each cluster, and ask whether they're useful for your goal. Clustering always returns clusters, even in random data."),
      ("k-means or DBSCAN?", "k-means for roughly round clusters when you know (or can choose) k. DBSCAN for irregular shapes, unknown k and when you want outliers flagged."),
      ("Why do I get different results each run?", "k-means depends on its random starting centroids. Use k-means++ and several restarts (n_init) and fix random_state for reproducibility.")],
 17: [("Does PCA throw away information?", "Yes, the variance in the dropped components — by design, usually a small amount. The explained-variance ratio tells you how much you kept."),
      ("Should I always use PCA before a model?", "No. It helps with many correlated features, visualisation or speed. With few features, or when interpretability matters, it's often unnecessary."),
      ("PCA or t-SNE?", "PCA for compression and as input to models; t-SNE (or UMAP) only for exploring a 2D picture.")],
 18: [("How many layers and neurons should I use?", "Start small (one or two hidden layers), check for underfitting or overfitting, and grow or regularise from there."),
      ("Why ReLU instead of sigmoid in hidden layers?", "ReLU is cheap and its gradient doesn't shrink for positive inputs, so deep networks train much better."),
      ("Is a neural network always better?", "No. On small tabular datasets, gradient boosting or logistic regression is often more accurate and far simpler.")],
 19: [("Do I need a GPU?", "For training large networks on images or text, yes in practice. For small experiments and for fine-tuning small pretrained models, a CPU can be enough."),
      ("What's the difference between a CNN and a Transformer?", "CNNs look at local patches with shared filters — ideal for images. Transformers let every position attend to every other — ideal for sequences like text, and now also used for images."),
      ("Should I train from scratch?", "Rarely. Fine-tuning a pretrained model needs far less data and computing power.")],
 20: [("How much time goes into data preparation?", "In real projects, usually more than into modelling. Clean, well-understood data matters more than the choice of algorithm."),
      ("Should I always build new features?", "Only if they add new information. Test each one with cross-validation — Exercise 4 in this lesson shows a feature that doesn't help."),
      ("What should I learn next?", "Practise on real datasets end to end, then go deeper into whichever area interests you: deep learning, time series, or the maths.")],
}
